<%#
  connect-stripe.ejs — Screen 2, Connect Stripe (AS-70).

  Built to docs/design/wireframes/screen-2-connect-stripe.html and its ledger,
  02-states-ledger.md §2. Every element the wireframe marks `data-wf-note` is
  wireframe annotation and is deliberately NOT built (01-screens.md §6).

  PRESENTATION ONLY. Every decision was made in lib/screens/connect-view.js;
  this file branches on precomputed nulls and reads properties. It does NOT
  branch on state ids: the locals already carry the copy, so there is no
  `state === '…'` here to drift from the view model. Three properties hold
  over it, each a concept row in test/dependency-policy.test.js rather than a
  convention (README.md § The view layer):

    P1  no raw-output tag anywhere — every interpolation is escaped by EJS
    P2  no interpolation in an href/src/action/formaction/style attribute, no
        event-handler attribute, no script or style element
    P3  every attribute value carrying data is DOUBLE-quoted, because escaping
        the double quote only helps if the double quote is the delimiter

  THE CLASS NAME IS A CONSTANT PER BRANCH, never built from the banner's tone.
  An interpolated class would sit inside a double-quoted value and pass P1-P4,
  but it would let copy-shaped data select a stylesheet rule; three literal
  branches keep the escaping surface to four sites — title, lede, the banner
  message and the button label — all element content, all renderer-authored
  constants. It is also what makes each rendered state own one distinctive
  marker in this source, which test/screens.test.js counts (plan §3.3).

  NO PARTIALS, deliberately: an include is a raw-output tag and would make P1
  conditional. The duplicated head block is the price, and the assertion that
  every template links both stylesheets and carries the viewport meta is what
  protects it.

  NOTE FOR ANYONE EDITING THIS COMMENT: do not spell the raw-output tag or a
  closing EJS tag inside it. The dependency-policy scanner strips an EJS comment
  up to its FIRST closing tag, so a spelled-out example would both truncate the
  strip and plant a P1 hit in a file whose whole job is not to have one.

  READY RENDERS ONE CONTROL, since AS-48: the wireframe's "Continue to
  Dashboard", a constant href to `/` (so P2a is untouched), inside the success
  banner's branch — the one branch only S2-RETURN-READY reaches, so no state
  id is consulted. Until screen 3 existed the anchor was withheld, because a
  link to `/` landed the freelancer back on this page (AS-70, plan §3.4).
-%>
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-state="<%= state %>">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Invoicing — <%= title %></title>
    <%# The vendored single source of visual truth, served byte-identical by
        routes/assets.js. Never a local copy. %>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/tokens.css" />
    <%# App-owned styles, served from public/ by express.static. %>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/app.css" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <%# Reduced chrome: no nav. Mid-onboarding, nowhere authenticated to
        navigate to yet (01-screens.md §3). %>
    <header class="site-header">
      <div class="container site-header__inner">
        <span class="app-label">Invoicing</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main class="container">
      <h1 class="page-title"><%= title %></h1>
      <% if (lede !== null) { %>
        <p><%= lede %></p>
      <% } %>

      <% if (banner !== null) { %>
        <% if (banner.tone === 'success') { %>
          <div class="banner banner-success">
            <p><%= banner.message %></p>
          </div>
          <div class="form-actions">
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/">Continue to Dashboard</a>
          </div>
        <% } %>
        <% if (banner.tone === 'warning') { %>
          <div class="banner banner-warning">
            <p><%= banner.message %></p>
          </div>
        <% } %>
        <% if (banner.tone === 'error') { %>
          <div class="banner banner-error">
            <p><%= banner.message %></p>
          </div>
        <% } %>
      <% } %>

      <%# The action is a CONSTANT, never an interpolation (P2a). One control,
          one route: POST /connect-stripe/start creates, resumes, or
          short-circuits, depending on the row — routes/connect.js decides. %>
      <% if (action !== null) { %>
        <form method="post" action="/connect-stripe/start">
          <div class="form-actions">
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary"><%= action.label %></button>
          </div>
        </form>
      <% } %>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
